docs/requirements/decisions/proof-or-stop-thin-entrypoint-contract-migration-execution-plan.md:256:4. For writes, keep the verified parent descriptor open; create a mode-`0600` exclusive no-follow temporary file in that same directory, write and fsync it, verify its identity, then `renameat`/platform-equivalent atomically within that directory and fsync the parent. Reopen the target no-follow, verify it is the same committed file and digest, and revalidate the parent chain before returning success. Cleanup uses the same descriptor-bound rules.
docs/requirements/decisions/proof-or-stop-thin-entrypoint-contract-migration-execution-plan.md:278:- Preferred developer storage is the OS keychain/credential store. The bounded fallback artifact ID is `thin_entrypoint_signing_private_key`, with the only allowed path `.lattice/keys/process-evidence/<signer>/<keyId>.ed25519`; it has an anchored registry matcher, is classified as a secret runtime artifact, is gitignored, is created exclusively with file mode `0600` and parent directories `0700`, and is accessed through the same symlink-safe no-follow I/O rules. No directory-wide private-key discovery is allowed.
docs/requirements/decisions/proof-or-stop-thin-entrypoint-contract-migration-execution-plan.md:280:- An external CI secret-file path is accepted only beneath an explicitly configured approved secret-mount root. The provider opens that root by walking from the filesystem/volume root component by component with `lstat` plus `openat(O_NOFOLLOW|O_DIRECTORY)` or the platform-equivalent reparse-point-safe handles, then walks every child component relative to retained directory descriptors. During this first walk it records the ordered identity of every opened parent descriptor and the final file: POSIX implementations record at least `(st_dev, st_ino)` from `fstat`, while other platforms record the equivalent stable volume/file IDs. It rejects symlink/reparse parents and final targets, mount/root escape, traversal, and injected parent swaps; the final no-follow descriptor must `fstat` as a regular file with expected owner and mode `0600` (or the platform's equivalently exclusive ACL). The key bytes are read only through that retained final descriptor, so a pathname swap cannot redirect the read. After the descriptor-bound read and before returning any bytes, the provider performs a second independent root-anchored no-follow walk beginning again at the filesystem/volume root, records the same ordered parent-chain and final-file identities, and compares the entire chain with the first-walk record. A missing component or any parent/final identity mismatch causes the provider to zero/discard the buffered key bytes and fail closed; it never returns bytes from an attacker replacement. Endpoint-only `realpath`/`stat`, a pathname reopen, or a second walk rooted in retained first-walk descriptors is forbidden. If a platform cannot guarantee these semantics, the external file provider fails closed and CI must use the in-memory environment provider or an OS credential store.
docs/requirements/decisions/proof-or-stop-thin-entrypoint-contract-migration-execution-plan.md:341:| Point the CI file provider at a valid mode-0600 file, then use deterministic race hooks to rename and replace an ordinary approved-root parent directory, replace the final target with a different regular file, replace a parent with a symlink/reparse point, and pause at the relevant boundaries between first walk, descriptor-bound read, and second walk | The provider returns the stable original-inode bytes only when the second independent root-anchored walk proves the entire ordered parent chain and final identity unchanged; otherwise it zeroes/discards buffered bytes and fails before return. It never returns attacker replacement bytes; an incapable platform fails the file provider and the in-memory/OS provider remains available |
docs/requirements/decisions/proof-or-stop-thin-entrypoint-contract-migration-execution-plan.md:515:| Private-key lifecycle and recovery | Exercise fresh human/bootstrap generation plus public-key commit, OS-provider stub, exact mode-0600 fallback and restart, missing/mode-0644/symlink/mismatched keys, CI secret-file/env injection, key loss, and two-step replacement | Valid providers derive the registered active public key and sign without logging/exporting secrets; invalid providers block signing with reason codes while public-key verification still works; no network KMS is contacted; recovery requires human pending→active registry commits and a fresh chain | Correct permissions/provider mapping or provision a new key, commit public pending then active/old retired, and regenerate readiness at the new HEAD | Stories 1, 3, 6, and 7 |
